      "claim": "Salmon (genus Salmo and Oncorhynchus) are anadromous fish that migrate from marine waters to freshwater to spawn.",
      "evidence": "Standard ichthyology textbooks and peer‑reviewed reviews of salmonid life cycles describe all true salmon as anadromous.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "All salmon species (including brown‑trout subspecies) perform fertilisation and egg deposition only in freshwater (rivers, streams, or lakes), never in saltwater.",
      "evidence": "Empirical studies of Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar), Pacific salmon (Oncorhynchus spp.) and brown trout show spawning occurs on freshwater substrates; no marine spawning has been documented.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The Caspian Sea is a landlocked, endorheic basin with no natural connection to the oceans where true salmon live.",
      "evidence": "Geographic data: the Caspian Sea is an enclosed inland body of water surrounded by land, lacking any navigable outlet to the Atlantic or Pacific.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The fish historically called “Caspian salmon” (Salmo trutta caspius) is a subspecies of brown trout, not a true salmon, and it is anadromous, entering the Caspian Sea for feeding but returning to tributary rivers to spawn.",
      "evidence": "Recent taxonomic revisions and IUCN assessments reclassify Salmo caspius as Salmo trutta caspius; life‑history studies show spawning occurs in freshwater rivers.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "No self‑sustaining populations of true salmon (Salmo salar or Oncorhynchus spp.) exist in the Caspian Sea; fisheries surveys list only sturgeon, carp, and trout.",
      "evidence": "Comprehensive FAO and regional fisheries surveys of the Caspian basin report absence of true salmon species.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Because spawning of salmon requires freshwater and there are no salmon that spawn in the Caspian Sea itself, no salmon mate (spawn) in the Caspian Sea.",
      "evidence": "Logical combination of steps 2, 4, and 5: spawning habitat is freshwater, and neither true salmon nor the brown‑trout subspecies spawn in the sea.",
